These are all things I consider outside of gameplay. (Yes, including
quit.) As to the point: conducts currently test discipline, which is
not limited to "intended discipline". E.g., you don't get "You
engraved something other than X, break illiteracy?" prompts--you just
don't do it in the game, whatever the specifics of your chosen
interface[1] to avoid it. That's a part of the game[2].

If the DevTeam did not intend this to be so, there would be
provisions. If by chance you wish to discuss this aspect of the game,
please keep in mind that:

1) It *is* an aspect of the game, whatever your understanding
of conducts is/was.

2) It has been thoroughly discussed, so you should check a group
archive first.

As to your example, that of braking wands: apply = break is not
intuitive. I think that's why there's a prompt. Quite likely for the
benefit of newbies. If there was a break command, there would be no
prompt.

[1] -- Note that confirmation dialogues are not solely part of the
interface. At least not in games such as NetHack.

[2] -- It's entirely your choice to change the game to something
easier, however--like a game prompting before conduct breaking. You
will not be playing the same game as the rest of us however.

> But you wouldn't be in favor of such a paranoid quit patch,
